# Remove every row of the four tables that held the sections of a chain:
# supermarket_item_sections, location_sections, section_categories and
# supermarket_sections (backend plan 0200, section 6). A section belongs to a
# shop since that plan. Its migration copied the selection of each shop into
# shop_sections, and nothing reads the four tables after it. The tables stay.
#
# THIS TASK IS ARMED FOR STAGING AND FOR PRODUCTION. It ships in the same
# release as the migration: check.sh passes while shop_sections does not exist
# yet, and post.sh, which runs after the migration, refuses to delete without
# it. A staging deploy until the first date below runs it. In production,
# release 0.14.0 runs it, and only until the second date. No other version runs
# it there. A deploy after a date records the task as `expired`, which is final.
#
# A window is at most 14 days after the commit that writes it, and both are at
# that limit. If release 0.14.0 is published after 2026-10-28, move the dates in
# a new commit BEFORE that release, and move PRODUCTION_RELEASE with them if the
# version changed. A date that is moved after it passed does not revive a task
# that a deploy already recorded as expired.
# The deploy unit whose release runs this task (k8s plan 0015 D9).
UNIT="velista"
RUN_UNTIL_STAGING="2026-10-28"
RUN_UNTIL_PRODUCTION="2026-10-28"
PRODUCTION_RELEASE="0.14.0"
DATABASES="catalog"
#
# The most rows the four tables may hold together for this task to start.
# check.sh counts them and refuses above this number.
#
# NEITHER CLUSTER WAS READ WHEN THIS WAS WRITTEN. The developer's own catalog,
# from which the first catalog of both clusters came, held 38 rows on
# 2026-10-14: 19 sections and 19 selections of the one shop that was walked, no
# category and no pin. A walked shop adds about 40 rows, and the old code keeps
# writing them until this release. 500 leaves room for a dozen walked shops. A
# cluster above it holds sections that nobody expected, and that is a question
# for a person. The check phase of a deploy prints the count it found.
MAX_ROWS_STAGING="500"
MAX_ROWS_PRODUCTION="500"
